      "description": "In frontend/src/router.ts, modify the beforeEach guard's Rule 1 so that logged-in users navigating to the named error routes ('not-found' and 'forbidden') are NOT redirected to /dashboard. Currently Rule 1 reads: `if (!to.meta.requiresAuth && isLoggedIn.value) { if (to.query.force === '1') { next(); return } next({ path: '/dashboard' }); return }`. Change it to additionally allow through when `to.name === 'forbidden' || to.name === 'not-found'`. Recommended implementation: extend the existing escape-hatch condition so the early `next()` fires for any of (a) `to.query.force === '1'`, (b) `to.name === 'forbidden'`, (c) `to.name === 'not-found'`. Example: `if (!to.meta.requiresAuth && isLoggedIn.value) { if (to.query.force === '1' || to.name === 'forbidden' || to.name === 'not-found') { next(); return } next({ path: '/dashboard' }); return }`. Do NOT change meta.requiresAuth on /403 or the catch-all (both must stay false so unauthenticated users can still see error pages directly). Do NOT alter Rule 2, Rule 3, or any other route definitions. After the change, verify by reading: (1) logged-in STUDENT typing /admin -> Rule 3 redirects to name:'forbidden' -> Rule 1 allows it through -> ForbiddenView renders. (2) logged-in user typing /this-does-not-exist -> matches catch-all (name:'not-found') -> Rule 1 allows it through -> NotFoundView renders. (3) unauthenticated user typing /403 or /bogus-path -> still sees the error page (Rule 1 short-circuits because isLoggedIn is false). (4) logged-in user typing /login -> still bounces to /dashboard (the named-route exception does not apply). (5) ?force=1 escape hatch still works.",
